Date: Wed, 17 Nov 1993 19:47:48 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: E-prime

Thanks to everyone who answered my message on E-prime.  For now I will
make do with a definition of E-prime that incorporates elements from
Nick's discussion:
	E' concerns itself mostly with the form "is <noun>", as
	in "A is A" (the ultimate egregious violation of E'),
	and "is <adjective>" where it can be converted to an
	action verb (eg "is red" --> "looks red").  
and Craig's:
	You can always translate passive voice to active, by naming the
	actors, and usually make your writing more clear in the process....
and that of someone who pointed out in a private message that in "is
written", "is" only functions as an auxiliary verb forming the passive
voice of "written", and thus need not be considered a violation of the
rules of E-prime.

(Mind you, I found the statements of others valuable in that they
stated these and other points in various terms so I could see them
from different points of view; these just seemed the clearest and most
basic.) 

I felt embarrassed that I didn't see the obvious solution of rewriting
"[The book] is written in E-prime." in the active voice -- a simple
expedient which I learned in high school and got out of the habit of
using while writing philosophy papers. 

Craig also says:
	Actually, if you can hack Heidigger, Korzybski might not seem hard.

I don't know about this -- I saw a copy in a used bookstore and my
head spun just looking at a couple of pages.  For the money, and
because the list of books I want to read is growing much faster than I
can read them, I decided not to buy it.  I think the layman's
knowledge I'm picking up will serve me for the time being, and I have
some Hayakawa (another General Semanticist, by report) on my "to read
soon" shelf (much more easily digested than Korzybski _or_ Heidegger,
from a brief look).  I just want enough to work with that I can feel
I'm playing Ellis's game in good faith.  He manages to get rid of
_all_ his "to be"s -- no mean task.  Try it sometime; if you don't
find writing that way enough of a challenge, try holding spoken
conversations with _no_ "to be"s in them -- no progressives, no
compound verbs, none.  I tripped over myself in almost every sentence
for about an hour and a half, then gave up.  I may find it easier once
I've had more writing practice. 

The difficulty with progressives ("I am going", "He was saying", etc.)
struck me as suspicious when I woke up this morning and thought about
how "to have" and "to be" _both_ have irregular construction in every
known natural language (from what I hear), and wondered why "is going"
would warrant any different treatment than "has gone" (Equality for
All Auxiliary Verbs!).  I feel relieved and gratified that I can
achieve consistency and still follow the rules of E-prime (as
expounded by list members) by including both rather than by excluding
both.

Perry writes:
	> 	2) "What are you doing?"

	Describe your current activity, (if you are trying to ask what
	someone's activity is) or Do you understand the implications of your
	actions? (if you are exclaiming that in the "Oh my god! What are you
	doing!" sort of way).

It occurred to me this morning that this "question" really has an
imperative meaning:  people use it to mean "Stop doing that!" when
knowing what you are doing has little or no value for them.  I prefer
that people say what they mean (this may have to do with my put-on
defenses being so weak).

BTW -- does Korzybski have as much of an argument with "to become" and
"to exist" as he has with "to be"?

Kennita

