Date: Wed, 17 Nov 93 19:51:55 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Drugs

> From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@cs.umb.edu>

> I took MMDA  (Ecstasy) in the early 80's several times. There have
> been studies that show that this drug can cause permanent neural
> damage after a few doses.

MDMA, actually -- MMDA is also interesting, but it's different stuff.
An executive summary of the neurotoxicity issue: high-dose
intravenous MDMA causes axonal degeneration in the serotonergic
system.  It is unclear whether this is reversible in monkeys as it
apparently is in rats.  It is further unclear what its effects are,
as no behavioral correlates have been found even in animals with
gross 5HT (serotonin) depletion.  Dosage schedules which mimic human
use, e.g.  Ricaurte's still-unpublished-dammit study for the IND
(yup, MDMA is in the FDA approval pipeline), do not produce this
effect.  For comparison, the anorexiant fenfluramine has been widely
and chronically used by large numbers of people.  It shows greater
`neurotoxicity' than MDMA in animal models, yet no clinical sequelae
have been reported.

I think it's unlikely that moderate use of MDMA will cause
significant neurotoxic effects.  However, this isn't enough
certainty for my taste.  If I found it helpful in a therapeutic
setting, I'd use it, as the benefits there are substantial.  But as
it stands (particularly given the circumstances of the black
market), I'll pass.  Those who choose otherwise would do well to
limit their total intake, which is a crucial variable in animal
models.  Under no circumstances would I take MDMA more often than
once a week, or in doses higher than 200 milligrams, or in
combination with stimulants (which enhance both cardiac effects and
neurotoxicity).  Caveat ingestor.

> The last straw was when I woke up with one pupil bigger than the
> other; at the time I thought this was a sign of brain damage.

It often is.  Pupil dilation isn't really a serotonergic thing, but
in your situation I probably would have stopped taking anything
and maybe dropped by a neurologist if it persisted at all.

> I took LSD a few times and found it very educational, especially the
[...]
> another list) but I would NEVER take it now. I can't imagine taking a 
> gamble on having the kind of bad trip or brain damage that I saw
> around me.  

One possible cause of a bad trip is believing unpleasant myths about
the substance.  LSD is one of the most heavily studied medicinal
chemicals in existence, and nobody has found evidence that it causes
brain damage.  Nor, by the way, does it cause chromosome damage.
There are risks to its use, but brain damage is not one of them.  

LSD can precipitate psychosis in susceptible people.  This may only
occur in people to whom it would have happened anyway in time, but
later is better than sooner when it comes to psychosis.  Those with
a personal or family history of mental disorders should think long
and hard before taking it.

Taking LSD can result in a `bad trip', usually an anxiety reaction.
This is best handled by having a controlled environment, an
appropriate mental set, and an unaltered companion.  Or two, so one
can go out for Thai (food, that is).  This risk cannot be entirely
eliminated, but staying out of mini-malls and Diamanda Galas
concerts will go a long way.

Finally and most importantly, an LSD trip can be a profound
experience.  You may see things you'd rather not have seen.  You may
change the way you think or feel about things.  If this sounds like
something you don't want, don't do it.

> I remember taking a friend's kid brother to the hospital.
> He had supposedly taken DMA (not mmda) and he was about as functional
> as a scared chimpanzee.

Really, DMA?  If that's 2,5-dimethoxyamphetamine, I'd like to hear
about this.  The stuff's been sold, but it's pretty obscure --
probably because it's largely a stimulant with few perceptual
effects.

> any idea of what was really in those pills or caps or paper slips that
> we ingested. Maybe if you know the chemist and can inspect the lab...

Blotter acid is pretty safe.  Most chemicals are not active in doses
you can fit on a 36mm^2 piece of paper.  Also, there's no reason
reason to "cut" it with anything, and any cut would probably be more
expensive than LSD itself.  With other things (e.g. black-market
"MDMA"), I agree you're taking a significant risk.

This turned out to be pretty long, but what the hell.  It hasn't
been rehashed on the list in the past few years...

