Date: Thu, 18 Nov 93 00:03:00 EST
From: wit@MIT.EDU (paul whitmore)
Subject: Drugs: Recommended Reading

enough people have taken drugs to provide fairly detailed knowledge 
without needing to try them firsthand. one could also say the same about 
guidebooks and Italy.

The person who strikes me as the most balanced and concise writer on 
drugs is Lester Grinspoon, a Harvard Med School professor. 
Two of his books cover the two most interesting drugs, to my mind:
The Speed Culture
Psychedelic Drugs Reconsidered.

Speed/amphetamine gets a pretty poor health record. although it received 
incredible reviews from subjects who were given amphetamine. in a study 
where med students were given opium, or heroin, or speed, without being 
told what they were receiving, only the speed subjects said they would 
like to have another round. altho IQ test scores bump up on speed, the 
improvement is quite temporary, and the long range is not smart.

Psychedelic Drugs Reconsidered has compendious reviews of the 
literature, which is a true service. A lot of the publishing on 
mushrooms, lsd, peyote was neither succinct nor solidly committed to 
scientific principles. Leary and Lilly stand out, imo, as arrogant and 
ultimately irrelevant.

psychedelic drugs, on the other hand, are not irrelevant to ayone 
interested in the relation between mind, evidence, and world. 
who knows why society mobilized such intense propaganda against 
psychedelics, but the exaggerations and hysteria are documented by 
Grinspoon. do people still refer to lsd-damaged chromosomes? they did 
ten yrs ago when i was in college, and that study was virtually a hoax. 
lsd was directly dropped on chromosomes; certain irregs resulted. the 
media propagated this, without comparing what salt or pepsi might do in 
similar circs.

as i recall, Grinspoon's overall evaluation of lsd: the worst 
physiological response to lsd is a bad trip. ie, there are no documented 
longterm changes in any brain or other tissue.

Grinspoon has been a vocal advocate of decriminalization; that is 
another discussion, orthogonal to the issues here.

