X-reposting-policy: do not redistribute; for Extropians list only.

[Blacknet's gonna love this one, so let me state right up front:
"I am not now, nor have I ever been, a Communist." ;-)]

If you don't want to read an honest favorable opinion about drugs, delete
this message now; it will only anger you, and I'd rather be on friendly
terms with you, you who will still be my contemporary 100+ years from now :-).

 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

Disclaimer: What I write below is in no way intended to be an advocacy
  or encouragement of using any of these drugs.

First, let me state my "qualifications" :-)
About 10 years ago I was kind of a wild young man and I experimented 
with a variety of drugs (notably marijuana, lsd, cocaine, and ecstacy) 
and found their effects to be rather different from each other.  Thus, 
I find the term "drugs" to be extremely misleading ... they're _not_ 
"all alike".  Here's a brief summary of what their effects have been 
on me:

alcohol (ethanol) - I remember feeling "giddy", a little dizzy, a little
  wreckless (I wasn't driving though), my field of vision seemed to move
  in little jumps, I felt less conscious than usual, and I felt like
  exercising my strength (I almost broke the theater seat in front of me
  at the movie house).  All in all, I found it mildly pleasant ... and
  mildly nauseating.
amphetamine - energy and alertness ... for a full day.  I found myself
  "doing" alot of things (some useful, many "just to be doing something"),
  and I didn't eat (and felt no desire to) that day.  I don't remember
  if I was "frazzled" by the end of the day, but I suspect so.  I've
  heard that amphetamines are fairly destructive, so I have no real
  desire to try them again.
caffeine - my usual cycle: increased alertness at first, the inevitable
  urge to go to the restroom, mild contentment and cheerfulness, a slight
  "stressed" feeling, then a mild sleepiness! The drug has been reported 
  to have some undesirable side effects, but since I only have about two 
  cups a day, I'm probably okay.
cocaine - mild stimulation for 2-3 hours.  I went swimming for longer
  than usual (and found it more enjoyable), and felt noticably more 
  talkative than usual.  I found the experience to be pleasurable, but
  considering the high price, the risk of unknown impurities, and the
  CNS damage associated with it (at least in regular heavy usage) I
  choose not to use it.
ecstacy (methylene-dioxy-methamphetamine) - warm fuzzy feelings, empathy,
  sometimes felt very strongly.  Described by one person as "it makes
  you like people."  This is probably the most enjoyable one I've tried,
  but again, because there may be unknown impurities and because there
  is some evidence of minor synaptic damage (in regular heavy usage),
  I choose not to use it.  I've heard of non-damaging analogs, and if
  I could gain access to a known pure (and legal ;-) source, I'd probably
  want to try them.
kh-3 (procaine) - this one is sold as a "life extension drug", but I have
  noticed a definite pleasurable rush lasting for at least a few minutes.
marijuana (cannabis) - a feeling of mild euphoria, increased desire to
  move my body "sensually", loosening of thought (free-flow association),
  enhanced feeling of the "profundity" of such thoughts (but not 
  necessarily actual profundity :-), relative loss of short-term memory,
  enhanced sensory perception, and later a feeling of tiredness.  
  Sometimes I felt slightly groggy the next day (and thus began my
  caffeine addiction ;-).
psychedelics (lsd, psilocybin (mushrooms), peyote) - these amplify and
  deepen whatever state of consciousness you're in.  If it's joy, wonder,
  ecstacy, positive anticipation, etc., a peak experience is likely.  If 
  it's fear, suspicion, paranoia, depression, etc., a nightmarish experience
  is likely.  The use of psychedelics goes back quite far in human history,
  and the field is far too rich for me to go into here.  I'd recommend
  reading about shamanism, witchcraft (naturalistic, not "Satan worship"),
  and recent investigations of the ecstatic visionary experience (Grof, 
  Hofmann, Ram Dass, Leary, Wilson, etc.).  I tried them for spiritual 
  insight (which wasn't what I expected), visions (I got that), ecstacy 
  (got that too), and a couple of times because I was bored (which was a 
  mistake).
quaalude (methyl-quaalone) - made me feel like I was drunk, but without
  the dizziness. I, uh, felt particularly "wild" on this one, as if I'd
  been "unleashed", so when the thought popped into my mind that I could
  probably leave knuckle impressions in the cabinet door, I punched the
  door (and did indeed leave a nice little row of 4 impressions, without
  feeling any pain).  I figured that my "wrecklessness" on this particular
  drug was reason enough not to repeat the experiment!

Drugs I haven't experimented with:
barbiturate - both Marilyn Monroe and Jimi Hendrix died of overdosing
  on this one (combined with alcohol).  I have no desire to try it.
crack - Of all the idiocies and outrageous lies vomited forth by the
  Partnership for a Drug Free America, their comments on crack may well
  have the most truth to them (though "crack babies" are really almost
  all "alcohol/malnutrition babies").  This does not, of course, mean
  that I have any respect whatsoever for the PDFA.
heroin - I've heard it described by an ex-junkie turned naval officer
  as: "sexual orgasm times 10 stretched out over a period of 4 minutes".
  It's also supposed to be fairly addicting and often cut with 
  undesirable substances, and since I don't like needles anyway, I have
  no particular desire to try it.
ketamine - John Lilly has written about this one, and it has been used
  in astronaut training.  I don't know much about it.
nicotine - one of the most addictive and destructive drugs I know of
  (at least in smoked form).  I will _not_ try this one (though I fully 
  support people's right to use it if they wish, and I don't hold it 
  against them).
opium - I wouldn't even know where to look for this one!
pcp - has a pretty bad reputation.  I've no desire to try it.
tranquilizers (Valium, Librium) - since these are legal, surely someone
  you know (maybe even on this list) can tell you about them.
etc.

My current status: After all this experimentation, I found out something
a little shocking ... almost all these drugs are essentially irrelevant
to my life!  It's not that they're "good" or "bad", but "interesting
novelties which to me tend to distract more than they serve". I still
do use caffeine and a few basic "smart drugs".  Politically, I think the
"War on (people who use) Drugs" is an abomination that must be brought to
a halt; America can handle the re-legalization of drugs.  To give further
insight into my context, here's my basic "intellectual" progression:

0. B+ to A- student, who started looking for more in life.
1. Psychedelic and other mysticisms 
   (Transcendental Meditation, Casteneda, Rajneesh, etc.)
2. Intelligence boosting and creative problem solving
   (Wenger, Creative Problem Solving Institute)
3. General Semantics (books and seminars)
   (Korzybski, IGS, ISGS)
4. "Success Seminars"
   (aborted attempt at "est", Summit, etc.)
5. Psychedelics revisited (discovered their irrelevance to my life)
   (Leary, R.A. Wilson, Lilly)
6. Brief stint in the New Age
   (Hays, Buscalia, Whole Being weekend, seminars, sweatlodge, etc.)
7. Libertarianism (I'm still with it!)
   (LeFevre, Tannehills, general reading, L.F. Books, etc.)
8. Objectivism (books and tapes) (sparked interest in philosophy)
   (Rand and others)
9. Basic Philosophy! (just started, really)
   (Socrates (through Plato), Nietzsche)
   (looking forward to Aristotle, Locke, Hegel, Whitehead, etc.)

Kennita wrote:
| I associate drugs with lack of control, and I feel petrified at the
| thought of lack of control.

  I hope my related experiences assist you here.  One thing to keep
in mind: each particular drug has different effects.  The ones that I felt
the most willingness to "lose control" on were alcohol and methyl-quaalone.
The ones which loosened my mind were marijuana, the psychedelics, and
ecstacy (to a certain degree ... a loss of "guardedness" against others),
which is a form of "loss of control", but then, that's what I was seeking!
  I don't know you very well, so I'm sure not going to make recommendations
one way or the other.  I would guess that if "control" is a big thing
for you, then you'll probably want to stay away from the drugs I mentioned
in the previous paragraph, _especially_ the psychedelics.  Those are only
for people who are pretty casual and relaxed with life, and who are looking
for experiences of a "spiritual" nature.

| [...] the issue of drugs.  RAW seems to think highly of their use
| in Self Transformation.

  They're not for everybody, and I get the impression that some people
are much more suited for them than others ("them" being the primarily 
_psycho_active drugs).  I can see how some people could gain a great
deal of insight and good from them; and I can see how it would really
be a mistake for other people.  If fear is your predominant emotion
towards these compounds, don't do them.

| It seems particularly stupid to me to take drugs when around other 
| people taking them, because if I did something stupid like drinking 
| Liquid Drano (TM) or breaking furniture, people who would otherwise 
| have the presence of mind to stop me might not.

  I think Dr. Timothy Leary would agree with you (assuming that it is
psychedelics that you are talking about here).  He has always advocated
the presence of a qualified "guide" who is either straight or who has
taken only a much reduced dosage (to gain greater empathy).  Anyone
who is serious about such compounds will treat their usage with a great
deal of respect, and will ensure that conditions are right before
proceeding.

John (JOHN on HEx :-)


