Subject: Man was not meant to be immortal!

Man was not meant to be immortal. This is why I am not signed up for
cryonics. It is unnatural and against God's word. 

And you Cryopunks are immoral for spending your money on this
perversion when it could better be spent on meeting valid social
goals.

-----

Actually, it is true that I'm not signed up for cryonics, but for
other reasons (lack of faith in Alcor, procrastination, expectation of
"extra life" is, IMHO, too low, etc.).

Many of you know my current status on this, but I think you'll also
agree that I don't bog the list down with "arguing the basics" about
the putative immorality of cryonics. This is the point I think Ray and
others are making in arguing that "the basics" should probably not be
debated too often. 

Jay Freeman's "first Extropian squirrel" series was a tweak of List
tenets, but a useful one (though not everyone liked the formal
tone...). Such challenges of ideas seem fully consistent with
Extropian ideas.
 
On the other hand, Carol Moore's comments about the validity of
astrology, the role of the stars in shaping our lives, and the reality
of reincarnation...well, what more can I say? Thankfully we now have
::exclude files to handle such true believers.      

Someone entering the list with the apparent intention of arguing for
the existence of an immmortal soul or for the need for confiscating
the wealth of the rich, to name just two examples, would be viewed
correctly as a _disrupter_. 

The ::exclude function, "shunning," is probably sufficient to handle
this case as well. As with the Icelandic system we so often point to,
shunning can really work. 

I'm not arguing for censorship, for kicking folks off the list, or for
banning certain topics. In extremely serious cases, the owners of the
list may need to take some action, though.

The "safe haven" idea sounds like we're "intolerant"--the one
remaining social taboo in modern America. Rather, I still hold to the
"party" model: we're like an electronic version of the Extropoganza
party in August...an unruly guest who insulted the hosts, or severely
disrupted conversations with drunken, obnoxious behavior would
presumably be asked to leave.

This is why bars have "bouncers." And this is why in rare
circumstances the List management needs to "bounce" disrupters.

Obviously I am not referring to Lefty in this regard, who has
contributed much to the list, or even to Eric Raymond (who I locked
horns with but whom I mostly chose to ignore). Probably Pandit Singh
and James Donald are the only ones I would think merit bouncing. (Not
that I'm the judge.)

Our most important goal should be to increase the quality of the
debate or discussion on the List. I'm as concerned as anyone about the
gradual decline. The loss of Robin Hanson (to Caltech grad school
priorities) was one blow, the loss of Hal Finney will be another. Not
to single them out, but they often (Hal not recently) contributed
interesting analyses and stimulated debate.

We need some thinkers, some essayists. Not just "me too" comments and
typical BBS-style repartee. 

Even some judicious debating of the basics might be welcome. (Heresy!
Heresy!) Provided the topic is approached carefully and without the
intent of flaming and disrupting the list, why not? If the alternative
is banality, I'll take arguing the basics. I note that Charlie Stross
wrote several fine essays from a viewpoint that is not traditionally
libertarian, and his contributions were excellent. (Haven't seen much
of him lately, though...)

(I apologize for any choppiness or rambling here...I'm typing this on
my Powerbook 170 tied in to Netcom at only 2400 baud--the internal
modem--and the whole feel is different from my normally speedy 14,400
baud connection with my Mac IIci.)

So, about the evils of trying to thwart God's plan by unnaturally
extending lifespans beyond four scored and ten....

--Tim May

